  "worked_solution": "Given a geometric series, the sum of the first $N$ terms is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series and $r$ is the common ratio. In our case, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and $r=2.$\n\nWe must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\,120 & =5\\cdot {2}^{N-1} \\\\ 1\\,024 & ={2}^{N-1} \\\\ {2}^{10} & ={2}^{N-1} \\\\ 10 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=5,$ $r=2$ and $N=11$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{11} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1-{2}^{11}}{1-2} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1-{2}^{11}}{(-1)} \\\\  & =5({2}^{11}-1) \\\\  & =10\\,235.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
